    explanation: 'Differential privacy formally: a mechanism M satisfies ε-DP if for all datasets D, D\' differing by one record, and all outputs S: P(M(D) ∈ S) / P(M(D\') ∈ S) ≤ e^ε. For ε=0.1: e^0.1 ≈ 1.105, meaning any adversary\'s advantage in distinguishing whether a specific patient was in training is bounded to ~10.5% above random. This defends against: (1) Membership inference attacks (Shokri et al.), inferring training set membership; (2) Reconstruction attacks, directly reduces memorization. Limitations: (1) ε=0.1 is very strong DP but may significantly degrade model utility; (2) DP protects individual records but not group-level patterns; (3) Composition, multiple queries consume privacy budget. Source: Dwork & Roth "The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy"; CAISP Domain 2; "Deep Learning with DP" (Abadi et al., 2016).',
